     The yy key turns the SELECTION into a library stencil - the
     editor's face of vveellssyymm. It asks for the stencil's code, its
     designator prefix, the library file to append to, and the
     scale, then writes the selection out as an ordinary drawing
     and runs that command on it, so a stencil made here and one
     made from make(1) are the same bytes.  The NN net-name
     markers in the selection become the PINS, in z-order; pin
     types are set afterwards in ssyymmeeddiitt, because a type is a
     property of the library and a guessed one builds a wrong
     ERC.  A running vveelllluumm reads its libraries only at start-up,
     so the new stencil appears in the palette next time.


SSHHEEEETT SSEETTSS
     A sheet is a file; a drawing that spans sheets is a SET of
     numbered files (_a_m_p_1._d _a_m_p_2._d, ...).  The > key saves the
     current sheet and loads the next - past the last one it of-
     fers to create it, which is how a set grows - and < walks
     back.  The Frame stamp adds ``Sheet n/m'' when the file name
     carries a number.  Shell globs are the set syntax for the
     whole suite: vveellnneett aammpp?.dd computes nets per sheet and
     MERGES them BY NAME - a net named (by an N marker or
     GND/VCC) VBUS on sheet 1 is the same conductor as VBUS on
     sheet 3; unnamed nets stay sheet-local and print qualified
     (NET-2.3 = sheet 2, net 3).  vveellpplloott emits the form feed be-


