     ttwweeeenn sheets, and vveellnneett -bboomm yields ONE parts list for the
     whole set.  An off-page reference is the OFFP stencil with
     its value reading ``3'' and an N net name on its pin - the
     netlist merge makes it electrically true.

     A BUS is an idiom, not an object: draw the trunk as a BOLD
     wire - graphics, not electricity - and tap it with short
     wires carrying NN DD00 ...  NN DD77; same-named taps merge across
     the whole sheet set, which is what a bus MEANS.  vveellcchheecckk
     flags a net name appearing on fewer than two pins in the set
     - the typo'd bit (D3 on one end, D8 on the other) that eyes
     cannot see.

TTHHEE SSUUIITTEE
     vveelllluumm DRAWS, and does nothing else.  Everything the shop
     does WITH a drawing is a command of its own, over the same
     model and the same plain-text format:

              vveellpplloott     a drawing onto a page - Epson raster,
                          PostScript or HPGL, -ffiitt to the page or
                          -ttiilleed across as many as it takes
              vveellppiicc      a drawing as troff ppiicc source, for figures
              vveellddxxff      the border with the office CAD, both ways
              vveellnneett      what it is CONNECTED as: the netlist, the
                          parts list, a SPICE deck, canonical designators
              vveellcchheecckk    prove it - one finding per line, the exit
                          status the finding count, so make(1) gates on
                          it like cc -Werror.  -ssyymm judges the stencil
                          LIBRARIES the same way
              vveellddiiffff     what changed between two revisions, as a list
                          or as a marked-up DRAWING
              vveelliinnffoo     the takeoff, the set-wide Find, a library's
                          reference card, the set's contents page
              vveellssyymm      a drawing becomes a stencil
              vveellggrraapphh    x/y data becomes a drawing

     Not one of them is a graphics client, so the whole pipeline
     runs from mmaakkee(1), from a script, or down a serial line on a
     machine with no bitmap card.  That is the reason for the
     split: the editor needs a screen, and nothing else here
     does.

     They compose with each other, because the file name - is
     standard input everywhere:

                  velinfo -symsheet pid.sym | velplot -T ps -fit -
                  veldiff -mark amp.d.A amp.d | velpic -

     A lowercase option given to vveelllluumm itself is an error naming
     these commands, not a mode: the editor will not quietly do
     another program's job.

     The board reaches two of them directly - Print runs vveellpplloott
     on a snapshot of the live drawing, and Make Symbol runs vveell-
     ssyymm on the selection - so a page or a stencil made on the


